Make cython-blis cross-compile for iOS and Android: give BLIS's own object
builder the cross compiler and a portable kernel set, and stamp the iOS
deployment target onto the objects it compiles behind distutils' back.

cython-blis vendors the whole BLIS library under `blis/_src` and does NOT use
distutils to compile it. `ExtensionBuilder.compile_objects()` reads a
pre-generated recipe file, `blis/_src/make/<platform>-<arch>.jsonl`, and for
each entry in it runs the compiler by hand:

    <compiler> -c <source> -o <target> <flags> <macros> <includes>

Nothing from the environment's CFLAGS/CPPFLAGS reaches that command line, and
both `<platform>` and `<arch>` are worked out from the *build* machine. That
is the root of every change below.

Which `<platform>-<arch>` pair is in play matters for reading the patch.
`sys.platform` inside forge's crossenv is `ios` on the iOS slices and `linux`
on the Android ones, and setup.py maps anything that is neither win32 nor
darwin to `linux`; with hunk 2 pinning the arch to `generic`, every slice we
build — iOS and Android alike — uses `make/linux-generic.jsonl` and
`include/linux-generic/`. That is why only the `linux-generic` copy of
`blis.h` is touched here.

  1. Android pthread barriers (`bli_pthread.c` and
     `include/linux-generic/blis.h`). BLIS's `bli_pthread_barrier_*` wrapper
     has two implementations: on Apple and Windows it builds barriers itself
     from a mutex and a condition variable, everywhere else it forwards to
     the platform's POSIX `pthread_barrier_*`. The choice is made twice —
     the header picks the *type* (BLIS's own struct vs `pthread_barrier_t`),
     the .c file picks the *implementation* — so both `#if`s must be flipped
     together, or you end up with a struct nothing implements or functions
     operating on the wrong type. Adding `__ANDROID__` puts Android on the
     self-implemented path. Bionic only declares `pthread_barrier_*` from API
     24 (`__INTRODUCED_IN(24)` in the NDK's `pthread.h`), and forge's Android
     floor was API 21 when this hunk was written; it is API 24 today
     (`CrossVEnv.BASE_VERSION` in src/forge/cross.py, raised in d17ce03), so
     the hunk is belt-and-braces now rather than strictly required. The
     revision of this patch that predates the June 2026 refresh carried the
     comment "__ANDROID__ added since these types aren't available until API
     level 24", which is the original reason.

  2. `BLIS_ARCH=generic` and `BLIS_COMPILER=$CC`, set from setup.py itself so
     they apply however the build is invoked. `get_arch_name()` otherwise
     picks the kernel family from the build machine: on the `linux` path it
     reads `platform.machine()` (which crossenv reports as the *target*
     machine) and, for x86_64, probes the compiler for `-march=znver1` /
     `znver2` / `skylake-avx512`. Left alone that selects Cortex-A57
     assembly kernels for arm64-v8a and the AVX-512/Zen `x86_64` kernel set
     for the x86_64 slices — micro-kernels tuned for a CPU no phone or
     emulator is guaranteed to be. `generic` selects BLIS's portable C
     reference kernels, the only defensible choice when the target CPU is
     unknown at build time, and it is also what routes the build through the
     `linux-generic` files patched above. `BLIS_COMPILER` overrides the
     `"compiler": "gcc"` baked into every jsonl entry with forge's cross
     clang; note the line reads `os.environ["CC"]` unguarded, so the build
     now hard-requires CC in the environment (forge always sets it).

  3. `-mios-version-min` for the iOS slices. `build_object()` composes its
     command line from the jsonl entry alone, so the deployment target that
     reaches every other compile in the wheel — `-mios-version-min=13.0`, in
     the target sysconfig's CFLAGS and appended again to CPPFLAGS by forge's
     `compile_env()` — never reaches these objects. The `$CC` wrapper does
     not supply it either: it expands to `xcrun --sdk
     iphoneos${IOS_SDK_VERSION} clang -target
     arm64-apple-ios${IPHONEOS_DEPLOYMENT_TARGET}`, and neither of those
     variables is set anywhere in this repo, so the triple carries no version
     and clang falls back to its own default. The hunk re-derives the value
     from `sysconfig.get_platform()`, which under crossenv reads the target
     sysconfigdata and returns `ios-13.0-arm64-iphoneos` — four
     dash-separated fields, first `ios`, second the deployment target. Android
     returns a two-field `linux-<machine>`, so the guard skips it there.
     No error text is recorded for this hunk; it was added in the run-up to
     ec8d184, whose squashed log includes "All packages can be built for iOS,
     except 'blis'", and what it compensates for is legible from forge's own
     CPPFLAGS handling.

diff --git a/blis/_src/frame/thread/bli_pthread.c b/blis/_src/frame/thread/bli_pthread.c
--- a/blis/_src/frame/thread/bli_pthread.c
+++ b/blis/_src/frame/thread/bli_pthread.c
@@ -319,7 +319,7 @@

 // -- pthread_barrier_*() --

-#if defined(__APPLE__) || defined(_MSC_VER)
+#if defined(__APPLE__) || defined(_MSC_VER) || defined(__ANDROID__)

 // For OS X and Windows, we define barriers ourselves in terms of the rest
 // of the API, though for slightly different reasons: For Windows, we must
diff --git a/blis/_src/include/linux-generic/blis.h b/blis/_src/include/linux-generic/blis.h
--- a/blis/_src/include/linux-generic/blis.h
+++ b/blis/_src/include/linux-generic/blis.h
@@ -1537,7 +1537,7 @@ typedef pthread_cond_t         bli_pthread_cond_t;
 typedef pthread_condattr_t     bli_pthread_condattr_t;
 typedef pthread_once_t         bli_pthread_once_t;

-#if defined(__APPLE__)
+#if defined(__APPLE__) || defined(__ANDROID__)

 // For OS X, we must define the barrier types ourselves since Apple does
 // not implement barriers in their variant of pthreads.
diff --git a/setup.py b/setup.py
--- a/setup.py
+++ b/setup.py
@@ -21,8 +21,13 @@
 import sys
 import platform
 import numpy
+import sysconfig

 MOD_NAMES = ["blis.cy", "blis.py"]
+
+# Redirect the compiler to CC
+os.environ["BLIS_ARCH"] = "generic"
+os.environ["BLIS_COMPILER"] = os.environ["CC"]

 print("BLIS_COMPILER?", os.environ.get("BLIS_COMPILER", "None"))

@@ -199,6 +204,9 @@
         objects = []
         platform_arch = platform + "-" + py_arch
         compiler = self.get_compiler_name()
+        host_triplet = sysconfig.get_platform().split("-")
+        print("Host triplet:", host_triplet)
+
         with open(os.path.join(BLIS_DIR, "make", "%s.jsonl" % platform_arch)) as file_:
             env = {}
             for line in file_:
@@ -228,6 +236,8 @@
                 spec["flags"] = [
                     f for f in spec["flags"] if "visibility=hidden" not in f
                 ]
+                if len(host_triplet) == 4 and host_triplet[0] == "ios":
+                    spec["flags"].append(f"-mios-version-min={host_triplet[1]}")
